  <title xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Study on illicit financial flows</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2" xml:lang="EN">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will provide a study advisor at the Department of Economics at the University of Copenhagen, who will be associated with DERG&#x2019;s work on illicit financial flows and extractives and will undertake a comprehensive review of the research and policy literatures, and will carry out interviews with key stakeholders (national authorities and international organizations). The specific contributions of the study adviser will be in the area of development economics as well as institutional analysis.

The assignment will examine the scale of IFFs associated with the extractive sectors, with the aim of assessing their characteristics and trends. It will assess national and international initiatives in the area (e.g. the Kimberley Process for diamonds) as well recent proposals (such as the use of &#x2018;block chain&#x2019; technologies to monitor and certify clean value chains for gemstones, precious metals and ornamental minerals). It will assess recent efforts to improve the regulation of major commodity trading hubs.

The output of the research will be threefold: i.	A study structured as an academic working paper to document the main characteristics of the illegal trade and associated financial flows. ii.	A study structured as an academic working paper assessing the main policy issues and recommendations for further action. 
iii.	Four seminars or national and international meetings (including the UN and OECD) with national and international policymakers to disseminate findings.</narrative>
  </description>
